{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Enable a persistent host firewall on node1 that permits inbound SSH and TCP 8080 while rejecting unrelated inbound connections.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The active IPv4 and IPv6 INPUT paths allow loopback, established/related traffic, ICMP, new TCP 22, and new TCP 8080, followed by terminal REJECT rules. Both firewall services are enabled for normal runlevels. A bounded restart changed the boot identity, after which SSH and both complete rulesets recovered with unchanged persistent-file checksums. SSH was directly observed listening; port 8080 permission and rejection behavior are established by live rules rather than external connection probes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7e7b08d8f2974140","cmd-6a255f761c394019","cmd-87d04d9df8e64712","cmd-8711704ff1104855","cmd-25ff500d3f9244b0","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 were identical before and after the work and restart. The executor used the already-present CentOS 5 iptables stack; the recovered package was iptables 1.3.5, and the timeline contains no package installation or OS/kernel replacement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f800ce325e8b4ae3","cmd-90fbc1145ce647f6","cmd-25ff500d3f9244b0","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.86,"summary":"The substantive mutations were causally appropriate: cmd-72397bd049fd45a2 created rollback copies and wrote the required persistent rules; cmd-7e7b08d8f2974140 enabled and activated them; cmd-87d04d9df8e64712 performed the mandatory restart. Two minor retained residues remain: cmd-72397bd049fd45a2 created /etc/sysconfig/iptables.pre-task and /etc/sysconfig/ip6tables.pre-task as useful rollback safeguards, but no later command removed these task-specific backups; cmd-87d04d9df8e64712 created /tmp/antrieb-restart.log for bounded restart evidence, and the final lifecycle snapshot confirms that temporary log remained. No unrelated package, service, or security mutation is shown.","evidence_ids":["cmd-72397bd049fd45a2","cmd-7e7b08d8f2974140","cmd-87d04d9df8e64712","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes are satisfied. The firewall is active and persistent across a directly observed restart, and the legacy OS, kernel, and compatible iptables stack were preserved. The provider-wide restart protocol is strong: it includes a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, observed outage, changed boot identity independently corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered SSH, firewall services, rules, and persistent checksums. Hygiene is reduced only for retained task-specific backup files and the temporary restart log.","limitations":["No external client probe directly attempted TCP 8080 or an unrelated inbound port; those results are inferred from coherent live kernel rules, while SSH availability was directly observed.","The lifecycle package-health probe is unsupported on this legacy yum version, so general package consistency was not independently assessed.","The lifecycle socket collector returned an empty inventory both before and after execution, so listener assessment relies on executor netstat output.","The final existence of the two .pre-task firewall backups is inferred from their successful creation and the absence of a cleanup command; lifecycle snapshots did not inventory /etc/sysconfig."]}
